AB  - Improving irrigation efficiency is essential for sustaining greenhouse cucumber production in Saudi Arabia, where agriculture relies heavily on limited non-renewable groundwater resources. This study evaluated the combined effects of subsurface drip irrigation (SSDI) and regulated deficit irrigation on hydraulic performance, soil-water distribution, cucumber productivity, water-use efficiency, physiological response and economic return. A split-plot experiment with three replicates was conducted during a single growing season in greenhouses without supplemental lighting, using sandy soil containing 88.4-95.0% sand. Three irrigation levels (100%, 80%, and 60% of crop evapotranspiration, ETc) and three dripline types (XFD, UNIRAM, and REIN) were evaluated. Irrigation water had an ECw of 0.91 dS m-1 and a SAR of 4.5. Seasonal irrigation depths were 363, 294, and 226 mm, saving 18.8% and 37.7% water. Marketable yield declined from 99.5 t ha-1 at 100% ETc to 80.6 and 65.1 t ha-1 at 80% and 60% ETc, respectively, whereas irrigation water-use efficiency reached 72.0 kg m-3 at 60% ETc. XFD showed the highest hydraulic uniformity and generally maintained better crop performance. Leaf proline responses were dripline-dependent, with the highest concentration under XFD at 60% ETc. Under the experimental conditions, 80% ETc offered a practical compromise between yield maintenance and water saving. Under a fixed-water-budget scenario, reallocating water saved at 60% ETc increased the potential cultivated area and improved overall economic return, although this benefit was conditional on the availability of additional production capacity.
