KW  - quality control
KW  - M-X algorithm
KW  - online assessment
KW  - client-server
AB  - Crowdsourced User Acceptance Testing (UAT) provides cost-efficient evaluation by involving diverse testers, but it often suffers from inconsistent outcomes due to ineligible participants. Conventional quality control approaches are difficult to apply in UAT because tasks are subjective and typically lack predefined correct answers. This study addresses the challenge by empirically validating the M-X algorithm as a consistency-based mechanism to classify tester eligibility without relying on ground truth. A client-server system was developed using GraphQL as the API gateway and MongoDB for data storage, integrating the M-X algorithm to process tester responses. The simulation involved 24 participants with balanced prior experience in UAT and was conducted through five iterations using structured Gherkin-based tasks. Prior-experience validation served as the reference benchmark for evaluating classification outcomes. The assessment employed Confusion Matrix metrics mapped directly to research questions, including Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1 Score, and True Negative Rate (TNR). Results showed that the M-X algorithm achieved 79% accuracy, 82% precision, 75% recall, 78% F1 Score, and 83% TNR. These findings indicate strong effectiveness in minimizing false positives while moderately conservative in recall. The study concludes that the M-X algorithm is a promising quality control approach for subjective and explorative UAT scenarios, though complementary mechanisms may be needed to fully capture tester expertise in real-world deployments.
